{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"34f37543b8d453b8086b3921f098cb16aab3b6f33d293c10c3bbb991fa3f1572","slug":"index","location":"/index.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Preview","site":{"hide_outline":true},"open_access":true,"license":{"content":{"id":"CC-BY-4.0","url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","name":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","free":true,"CC":true},"code":{"id":"MIT","url":"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT","name":"MIT License","free":true,"osi":true}},"github":"https://github.com/underworld-technical-notes/underworldcode.org","source_url":"https://github.com/underworld-technical-notes/underworldcode.org/blob/main/index.md","edit_url":"https://github.com/underworld-technical-notes/underworldcode.org/edit/main/index.md","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"index.md","url":"/underworldcode.org-preview/6de493f15a/build/index-48325a582bdc00b214ff6c3e6e16af5a.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-masthead","children":[{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-kicker","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preview","key":"bJ0aSKOPYz"}],"key":"EKkytmJaKV"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-wordmark","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Under review","key":"xh5ag7u9bK"}],"key":"GKGlSdDBWl"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-standfirst","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Only the notes changed on this branch were built. The rest of the site is not here, which is why this was quick.","key":"m3yWtLqvUn"}],"key":"XvT2Sdj4BR"}],"key":"jXpxLa1GjS"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-feed","children":[{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry","children":[{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-text","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"children":[{"type":"link","url":"/faults-on-an-add-on-mesh/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Faults and patch-refinement of the mesh","key":"BMdUN4u6Z4"}],"urlSource":"/faults-on-an-add-on-mesh/","key":"bry9MZ1Z5u"}],"identifier":"faults-and-patch-refinement-of-the-mesh","label":"Faults and patch-refinement of the mesh","html_id":"faults-and-patch-refinement-of-the-mesh","implicit":true,"key":"QiNGsPxyGm"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-summary","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A slipping fault is a discontinuity the mesh has to represent, along a trace that moves as the model runs. We build the fault into a short-lived mesh made from a static base. The base holds the state, the partition and the coarse multigrid levels; the add-on is a copy of the base's finest level with the fault placed into it as a narrow structured band, split along the fault, and rebuilt from the base whenever the fault moves.","key":"P4tKOAGutN"}],"key":"ueSTM3NLYb"}],"key":"HromUm1sVM"}],"key":"cj9k983gir"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry","children":[{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-text","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"children":[{"type":"link","url":"/putting-a-fault-in-a-mesh/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Faults: to mesh or not to mesh?","key":"bP86eFxP98"}],"urlSource":"/putting-a-fault-in-a-mesh/","key":"pbSAp6WyGO"}],"identifier":"faults-to-mesh-or-not-to-mesh","label":"Faults: to mesh or not to mesh?","html_id":"faults-to-mesh-or-not-to-mesh","implicit":true,"key":"gVNNpeRvia"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-summary","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A fault is a discontinuity and a mesh represents continuity, so the first question is whether the fault has to go into the mesh at all. Shear bands and a painted director cost no mesh work; cutting and conforming cost a great deal. What each of the four representations asks of the mesh and of the constitutive model, where they agree, and why they part company at a junction.","key":"Wa7M3UX0Zm"}],"key":"GWMeoliONC"}],"key":"LvvQao3soI"}],"key":"SYl8cgpb8U"}],"key":"o48zLxCrWh"}],"key":"cvA6cPHvCC"}],"key":"SNwmgmWfoa"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"next":{"title":"Faults and patch-refinement of the mesh","url":"/faults-on-an-add-on-mesh","group":"Underworld Geodynamics"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}